1121":"child","H1637":"threshing floor","H854":"with","H3068":"LORD","H6635":"army","H3478":"Israel","H1746":"Dumah","H8165":"Seir","H8104":"obey","H4100":"what?","H857":"to come","H1242":"morning","H1571":"also","H518":"if","H1158":"to enquire","H7725":"return","H6152":"Arabia","H3293":"wood","H3885":"to lodge","H736":"caravan","H1720":"Dedanite","H7125":"befall","H6771":"thirsty","H4325":"water","H3427":"to dwell","H8485":"Tema","H3899":"food","H6923":"to meet","H5074":"to wander","H6440":"before","H2719":"sword","H5203":"to leave","H7198":"bow","H1869":"to tread","H3514":"heaviness","H4421":"battle","H5750":"still","H8141":"year","H7916":"hired","H3615":"finish","H3519":"glory","H6938":"Kedar","H7605":"remnant","H4557":"number","H1368":"mighty man","H4591":"to diminish","H1696":"speak"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","The desert of the sea. So Babylon is here called, because from a city as full of people as the sea is with water, it was become a desert. (Challoner) — After its fall, it was mostly inundated, chap. xiii. 20. — Land. Media and Persia, which lay to the south, and were not so beautiful as the environs of Babylon."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Spoileth. Baltassar is incorrigible, or his opponents must proceed. (Calmet) — Elam; that is, O Persia: (Challoner) Cyrus, and Darius, the Mede. (Calmet) — The former nation was weak, and the latter strong. (Worthington) — Cease. The enemy will shew no pity; nor shall I; as Babylon did not heretofore. (Haydock)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Pain. He bewails the crimes and the fall of Babylon, which at this time was in amity with Ezechias, ver. 10. (Calmet)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Babylon. Protestants, “the night of my pleasure hath he turned into fear unto me.” Septuagint, “My soul is turned into fear.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Drink. Persians refresh yourselves. — Take up. Hebrew, “anoint.” He may also allude to the Babylonians, who were feasting."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Camel. These two riders are the kings of the Persians and Medes. (Challoner) — The sentinel, placed by Isaias, in spirit, or rather by the king of Babylon, brings these tidings. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Out. Literally, “He cried, a lion.” (Haydock) — Cyrus appears like one. Septuagint, “And call Urias to the watch-tower,” &c., chap. viii. 2."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Horsemen, drawn by the ass and camel, ver. 7. This was verified long after."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Floor: you who must shortly be reduced to the utmost distress. Baladan was friendly to Ezechias. But Assaradon having seized Babylon, took Manasses prisoner; and the city thenceforward continued to fill up the measure of its sins. (Calmet)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Duma. That is, Idumea, or Edom. (Challoner) — It was a city of that country, twenty miles from Eleutheropolis. (St. Jerome) — Assaradon desolated Idumea the year following, ver. 16. The Jews absurdly apply to Rome what is said of Edom. (St. Jerome) (Calmet)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Night. Instead of joy, I must announce dreadful things. (Haydock)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Arabia. This sentence is not in the Roman (Calmet) or Alexandrian Septuagint, (Haydock) and Dedan is a city of Idumea. (Calmet) — The Ismaelites are threatened. (Worthington)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Water. To neglect this was to be accessary to another’s death, in those dreary regions, chap. xvi. 3., and Deuteronomy xxiii. 2."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Hireling; counting precisely, chap. xvi. 14. (Calmet) — Cedar: Arabia, (Challoner) near to Edom. (Calmet)"],["challoner",1,null,"challoner","The desert of the sea.... So Babylon is here called, because from a city as full of people as the sea is with water, it was become a desert."],["challoner",2,null,"challoner","O Elam.... That is, O Persia."],["challoner",7,null,"challoner","A rider upon an ass, etc.... These two riders are the kings of the Persians and Medes."],["challoner",8,null,"challoner","And a lion cried out.... That is, I Isaias seeing the approaching ruin of Babylon, have cried out as a lion roaring."],["challoner",11,null,"challoner","Duma.... That is, Idumea, or Edom."],["challoner",16,null,"challoner","Cedar.... Arabia."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[10,null,0,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 2",0,339,"\"Hear ye isles, which are forsaken and tortured, hear, what I heard from the Lord of Sabaoth: the God of Israel has announced (it) to us. Note the way in this passage also in which he does not call those of the Circumcision to hear the unspeakable words, but those only, whom he calls \"forsaken and tortured,\" as were those in the apostolic age who bewailed and lamented the evil of the life of men."],[11,null,1,"Morals on the Book of Job","Book06",1,604,"Again he says, \"Watchman, what of the night? Watchman, what of the night? The watchman said, 'The morning comes, and also the night.' \" For \"the watchman came by night,\" in that the guardian of the human race even showed himself manifest in the flesh, and yet Judea, being close pressed by the darkness of its faithlessness, never knew him. Where it is well added in the voice of the watchman, \"The morning comes, and also the night. For by his presence has a new light shone out upon the world, and yet the former darkness remained in the hearts of unbelievers. …"],[14,null,1,"Morals on the Book of Job","Book23",1,604,"Again, by bread is set forth the instruction of heavenly doctrine, as is said by the prophet, \"You who dwell in the land of the south, meet with bread him that is flying away. For they dwell in the land of the south who, placed within [the] holy church, are breathed upon by the love of the Spirit from on high. But he is flying who is wishing to escape from the evils of this world. …"]],"fau":[["eusebius","Eusebius of Caesarea",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,339],["gregory-great","Gregory the Great",1,"father",null,1,604]],"ftr":[["W. J. Ferrar, The Proof of the Gospel (SPCK, 1920)"],["Library of the Fathers, Morals on the Book of Job (1844-1850)"]]}